# id: M76-read-answered-on-the-quorum-alone
# covering-test: TestRaftExitCriteria
# package: ./sim/hunt/
# expect: killed
# power-seeds: 30
# power-detector: sweep
# power-measured: 1 of 30, first at seed 27, under CURRENT (A7's shape), with the clean tree at 0 of 30 and the mutation VERIFIED PRESENT before the sweep. It is caught by read-index-answers-match-the-log, and the verdict names the planted defect exactly: 'answered a read OFF THE LOG having confirmed it could serve at index 14, while its own state machine had only applied to 12'. The rate is low because the defect is only reachable on the FOLLOWER path -- on a leader the wait is held redundant by the drain loop applying before it serves -- and follower reads did not exist in the sweep until this phase added designated-replica dispatch. This class ALSO produced A7's sharpest instrument finding: its first kill carried a verdict describing state AHEAD of the confirmed index, which is not what it plants, and that discrepancy exposed the oracle comparing against Index rather than against AppliedAt -- it would have failed clean runs (DESIGN-A7 section 5b). An earlier ERROR from the gating lane was a dirty-tree artefact, not a property of the class
# mutates: a read is answered as soon as its leadership quorum confirms, WITHOUT waiting for this replica's own applied index to reach the confirmed read index. That is section 1.1's SECOND condition dropped, and it is the half that gets forgotten because the hard-looking half -- talking to a quorum -- is already done. The quorum establishes a POSITION: that this leader was still leader at or after the read arrived, so nothing a newer leader committed can be missed below that index. It says nothing whatever about whether THIS node's state machine has got there yet, and answering before it has is reading your own past. Ansh, ruling on the differential oracle: 'plant it -- a mutant that answers on the quorum alone, killed by the differential oracle, since a client-visible stale read is precisely what ruling 5's fixture exists to catch'. read-index-answers-match-the-log is that fixture: it replays the range's committed prefix to the confirmed index and compares, so it sees a stale answer whether or not any client observed the write that was missed -- which per-key linearizability cannot
--- a/store/node.go
+++ b/store/node.go
@@ -906,7 +906,7 @@
 func (n *Replica) serveReadyReads(at clock.Instant) {
 	kept := n.pendingReads[:0]
 	for _, q := range n.pendingReads {
-		if q.index == 0 || n.applied < q.index {
+		if q.index == 0 {
 			kept = append(kept, q)
 			continue
 		}
